\begin{tabu} to \linewidth {| >{\raggedleft\arraybackslash}X[0.1] | X[0.9] |}
    \hline
    \textbf{Topic:} & Towards a Faster FFT-Accelerated Full-Wave Solver and Its Integration into Shape Optimization Strategies \\ \hline
    \textbf{Synopsis:} & The method of moments formulation of the electric field integral equation leads to a linear system of equations that poses a serious problem for iterative solvers in the form of the low-frequency and dense-discretization breakdowns. We have developed such an iterative solver on a uniform rectangular grid, whose translation invariance gives the impedance matrix a Toeplitz structure, so that the matrix-vector product can be accelerated using the fast Fourier transform. This removes the memory and computational bottleneck, but the real issue remains the convergence rate and accuracy of the solver. Our previous attempts to improve it with circulant preconditioners have been only partially successful. This thesis will therefore propose a more powerful preconditioner based on the discrete sine and cosine transforms, which diagonalize the discrete curl and divergence operators on the rectangular grid. This allows the preconditioner to compensate for the opposite differential orders of the vector and scalar potential operators. To maintain the accuracy of the solver at very low frequencies, we will solve the system in an explicit loop/star basis in which the scalar potential acts only on the charges. Since the discrete sine and cosine transforms can be computed via the fast Fourier transform, the preconditioner can be applied in $\mathcal{O}(N \log N)$ time with $\mathcal{O}(N)$ memory, so the solver retains its original complexity. The performance of the preconditioner will be tested on the eigenvalue problem of the characteristic mode decomposition, which places high demands on its quality, and its strengths will be discussed. The second part of the thesis addresses the application of the solver to the optimization of end-fire arrays. \\ \hline
\end{tabu}

